      "text": "We are dealing with a circle with center \\(O\\) and diameter \\(AB\\). Since \\(E\\) is a point on the circle, the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are perpendicular to the radii at those points, implying those tangents are perpendicular to \\(OB\\) and \\(OE\\), respectively.\n\nGiven \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\), we are to find \\(\\angle CED\\).\n\nFirst, we identify the key properties and relationships:\n1. \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\) \n2. \\(\\angle BAE\\) is an angle in the circle, so the corresponding inscribed angle \\(\\angle ABE\\) is half of \\(\\angle BAE\\): \n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = \\frac{1}{2} \\angle BAE = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 43^\\circ = 21.5^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nNext, since \\(\\angle ABE\\) is an inscribed angle that intercepts arc \\(BE\\), we establish:\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAE - \\angle ABE = 180^\\circ - 43^\\circ - 21.5^\\circ = 115.5^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\angle ABE\\) is an inscribed angle, the intercepted arc \\(ABE\\) can be calculated by:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Arc } AB \\text{ equals } \\angle ABE = 115.5^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) is a point on the circle, the minor arc \\(AE\\) will be:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{Major Arc } ABE = \\text{Minor Arc \\(AE\\)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Minor Arc } AE = 180^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\text{Arc } ABE = 180^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 115.5^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 57.75^\\circ = 122.25^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n**Thus, we conclude:**\n   \\[\n   \\angle CED = 122.25^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nThe required angle \\(\\angle CED\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{122.25}\n\\]",
